    "title": "What should we do with all this stage 2 baby food now that my son is eating stage 3?",
    "body": "<p>My son recently started eating Stage 3 baby food - and this is great!</p>\n<p>But, now I have a whole lot of Stage 2 food left over, and I'm not sure what to do with it all.</p>\n<p>Should we continue to feed him some Stage 2 food in addition to his Stage 3 meals?  Or should we completely stop feeding him Stage 2?</p>\n"
  },
  "answers": [
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 3674367,
        "reputation": 72549,
        "user_id": 9327,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "accept_rate": 50,
        "profile_image": "https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256",
        "display_name": "anongoodnurse",
        "link": "https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"
      },
      "is_accepted": true,
      "score": 7,
      "last_activity_date": 1655476398,
      "creation_date": 1655476398,
      "answer_id": 42696,
      "question_id": 42695,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>There’s nothing nutritionally wrong with stage 2 baby food; it’s fine to use it up. It’s just that it’s not as interesting as stage three and doesn’t accustom the baby to more “real/solid” foods. But nutritionally, it’s like you eating soup instead of steak. It’s all good.</p>\n<p>Feel free to use it as is, or if you really want to keep pushing the envelope, incorporate it into more solid food: the puréed fruit and veggies can be incorporated into baked goods, deserts, etc. Your imagination is the only limitation.</p>\n<p>Of course there are always women’s shelters and food banks that would welcome baby food. Totally up to you.</p>\n"
    }
  ],
